${C_BOLD}suitehive${C_RESET} $SUITEHIVE_CLI_VERSION: install and run a self-hosted Suitehive
(Mail, Calendar, sign-in/SSO and Admin) with Docker.

${C_BOLD}Usage:${C_RESET} suitehive <command> [options]

${C_BOLD}Commands:${C_RESET}
  install      Set up Suitehive on this server (or --local to try it on a laptop)
  status       Show containers, health, version and URLs
  logs         Show service logs (logs [service] [-f])
  upgrade      Pull new images (or --version X) and restart
  backup       Write a backup archive (databases, mail blobs, settings)
  restore      Restore a backup archive
  dns          Print the DNS records to create, and check them (--check)
  uninstall    Stop and remove the containers (--purge also deletes data)
  version      Print the CLI, image and docker compose versions
  help         Show help for a command: suitehive help <command>

${C_BOLD}Global options:${C_RESET}
  --dir <path>   Install directory. Default: \$SH_DIR, ./ if it holds an install,
                 /opt/suitehive as root, ~/suitehive otherwise
  --yes, -y      Never ask; accept defaults and confirmations
  -h, --help     Help for the command

Environment: SH_DIR, NO_COLOR, SH_REF / SH_REPO_RAW (where templates come from).
Docs: https://github.com/Suitehive/suitehive
EOF
}

help_install() {
  cat <<EOF
${C_BOLD}Usage:${C_RESET} suitehive install [options]

Installs Suitehive into the install directory and starts it: writes .env (with
freshly generated secrets), docker-compose.yml, Caddyfile and initdb/, pulls the
images, starts the stack, creates your organization and first admin, and prints
the DNS records to create.

Asks a few questions when run in a terminal; every answer can also be given as
a flag (or SH_* environment variable) for unattended installs. Re-running it on
an existing install keeps the existing secrets and applies new settings.

${C_BOLD}Server install:${C_RESET}
  --domain <name>          Mail domain, e.g. example.com            (SH_DOMAIN)
  --hostname <name>        Public hostname [suite.<domain>]         (SH_HOSTNAME)
  --admin-email <addr>     First admin, on <domain> [admin@<domain>] (SH_ADMIN_EMAIL)
  --admin-name <name>      Admin display name [Admin]               (SH_ADMIN_NAME)
  --admin-password <pw>    At least 12 characters. Default: generated and shown
                           once; never written to disk          (SH_ADMIN_PASSWORD)
  --org-name <name>        Organization name [from the domain]      (SH_ORG_NAME)
  --tls-email <addr>       Let's Encrypt account email [admin email] (SH_TLS_EMAIL)
  --version <tag>          Image tag [$DEFAULT_IMAGE_TAG]                         (SH_VERSION)

${C_BOLD}Modes:${C_RESET}
  --local                  Try it on a laptop: http://localhost:8080, no TLS, demo
                           org and users (domain $DEMO_DOMAIN), mail ports
                           2525/2465/2587/1993
  --build                  Build the images from source (clones the Suitehive repos
                           into <dir>/src; needs git)

${C_BOLD}Behavior:${C_RESET}
  --dir <path>             Install directory (see \`suitehive help\`)
  --yes, -y                No questions; accept defaults
  --dry-run                Write all files and show the docker commands, the
                           setup request (password masked) and the DNS records,
                           without running anything in Docker
  --force                  Replace an existing .env, generating NEW secrets (only
                           for a fresh start: existing data becomes unreadable)
  --skip-checks            Continue on a server with less than 4 GB of memory

${C_BOLD}Examples:${C_RESET}
  suitehive install
  suitehive install --domain example.com --admin-email founder@example.com --yes
  suitehive install --local
EOF
}

