  postgres.sql.gz     pg_dumpall of all Suitehive databases
  mailblobs.tar.gz    stored message bodies and attachments
  config/             .env, docker-compose.yml, Caddyfile, initdb/

The stack must be running. The archive contains your secrets (.env) and all
mail: it is created with mode 600; store it somewhere safe and private.
EOF
}

help_restore() {
  cat <<EOF
${C_BOLD}Usage:${C_RESET} suitehive restore <file.tar.gz> [--keep-env] [--yes]

Replaces ALL current data with the contents of a backup archive:
  1. asks you to type the domain to confirm (or --yes)
  2. restores .env from the archive (current one kept as .env.before-restore-*),
     unless --keep-env
  3. stops the app services, recreates the databases and loads the dump
  4. restores the mail blobs and starts everything again

Works on a fresh server too: point --dir at an empty directory and the
configuration comes from the archive.
EOF
}

help_dns() {
  cat <<EOF
${C_BOLD}Usage:${C_RESET} suitehive dns [--check | --no-check] [--token <admin token>]

Prints the DNS records your mail domain needs (A/AAAA, MX, SPF, DMARC,
autodiscovery SRV, domain verification) and, when dig or nslookup is available,
looks each one up and marks it found ($G_OK), missing ($G_BAD) or different ($G_NE).

DKIM (sh1._domainkey.<domain>) is generated after the domain is verified. Pass an
admin API token (--token, or SH_ADMIN_TOKEN) to print it here, or open
Admin -> Domains.

Set SH_PUBLIC_IP / SH_PUBLIC_IP6 to skip public IP detection (use "none" to
leave that record out).
EOF
}

